%description:

RFC 6298 -- the timeout is three seconds when data starts after a lost SYN.

Checks RFC6298-SYN-1 (must).

RFC 6298 section 5.7: when the timer expires while the sender is waiting for the
acknowledgment of a SYN, and the timeout in use is below three seconds, the timeout is set
to three seconds when the data transfer begins. This is the one rule RFC 6298 added over the
document it replaced, so a model written against the older text is likely to keep whatever
value it had.

**How the rule is observed.** The written procedure reads the timeout of host A at the first
data send. The model publishes no signal that carries it: `TcpBaseAlg` emits `rto` only from
`receivedDataAck`, which has not run at that point in this scenario, because nothing has been
acknowledged yet. The timeout is read behaviourally instead. The relay discards the first
data segment as well, and the interval until host A sends it again **is** the timeout that
was in force at the first data send.

The scenario needs both losses on one path, which is two rules on one relay: the first SYN,
and then the first data segment. Each rule counts its own occurrences, so neither shifts the
other's numbering.

**The model conforms.** The run drops both frames and host A retries the SYN:

    PacketTap dropping SYN (selected #1)
    PacketTap dropping tcpseg(l=500) (selected #2)

and the interval to the retransmission of the data segment is three seconds. The model
reaches the value RFC 6298 section 5.7 demands, although it reaches it for a different
reason than the rule gives: three seconds is the model's timeout from the start, the default
of `rexmit_timeout` in `TcpBaseAlgState.msg`, and the SYN loss does not lower it. A model
whose initial timeout were below three seconds would have to raise it here, and this check
would then be measuring the rule rather than the default. That distinction belongs in the
results, not in the verdict: the observable the standard names is the value, and the value
is right.

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"

%global:

#include <cmath>

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

// A pure acknowledgment carries the same port and no SYN, so a step that wants a data
// segment says so.
static bool dataSegment(const MatchContext& context)
{
    return context.event.packet != nullptr && context.event.packet->getByteLength() > 100;
}

// RFC 6298 section 5.7: three seconds, and this check reads it as an interval.
static bool threeSecondsAfterTheDataSend(const MatchContext& context)
{
    double sentAt = context.get("dataSentAt").doubleValue();
    double interval = context.event.time.dbl() - sentAt;
    return std::fabs(interval - 3.0) <= 0.01;
}

static cValue eventTime(const PacketEvent& event) { return cValue(event.time.dbl()); }

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    // Two rules on one relay. The first loses the SYN, the second loses the first data
    // segment so that the timeout in force can be read as an interval.
    return ProtocolTest("rfc6298_timeout_after_lost_syn")
        .intercept(tap("relay")
                       .filterPacket("tcp.synBit == true && tcp.ackBit == false")
                       .nth(1)
                       .drop().describe("host A's first SYN"))
        .intercept(tap("relay")
                       .filterPacket("tcp.destPort == 1000 && tcp.synBit == false")
                       .minBytes(100).nth(1)
                       .drop().describe("host A's first data segment"))
        // The retransmitted SYN opens the connection, so the handshake completed after a
        // loss, which is the condition the rule is about.
        .once(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetReceivedFromLower")
                  .filterPacket("tcp.synBit == true && tcp.ackBit == true")
                  .describe("the SYN+ACK, after the first SYN was lost")
                  .within(20.0))
        // The data transfer begins. Remember the moment.
        .once(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .filterPacket("tcp.destPort == 1000 && tcp.synBit == false")
                  .filterEvent(dataSegment)
                  .capture("firstSeq", "tcp.sequenceNo")
                  .capture("dataSentAt", eventTime)
                  .describe("the first data segment after the handshake")
                  .within(20.0))
        // It is lost, so host A sends it again after the timeout that was in force, which
        // RFC 6298 says must be three seconds.
        .once(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .filterPacket("tcp.sequenceNo == {firstSeq} && tcp.synBit == false")
                  .filterEvent(dataSegment).first()
                  .assertEvent(threeSecondsAfterTheDataSend)
                  .describe("the retransmission, three seconds after the first data send")
                  .within(30.0));
}

%activity:
// present only so opp_test compiles the %global above; this module is not in the network.
EV << "rfc6298_timeout_after_lost_syn compiled\n";

%file: test.ned

import inet.node.inet.StandardHost;
import inet.node.ethernet.Eth100M;
import inet.networklayer.configurator.ipv4.Ipv4NetworkConfigurator;
import inet.protocoltest.PacketTap;

// Two hosts with a relay on the link -- note: NO tester (attached at runtime).
network LostSynNet
{
    submodules:
        configurator: Ipv4NetworkConfigurator { @display("p=100,50"); }
        host1: StandardHost { @display("p=300,100"); }
        relay: PacketTap { @display("p=400,100"); }
        host2: StandardHost { @display("p=500,100"); }
    connections:
        host1.ethg++ <--> Eth100M <--> relay.a;
        relay.b     <--> Eth100M <--> host2.ethg++;
}

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
network = LostSynNet
sim-time-limit = 60s
seed-set = 0

# static ARP: the only frames on the wire are the TCP/IP ones.
**.ipv4.arp.typename = "GlobalArp"

*.host1.numApps = 1
*.host1.app[0].typename = "TcpSessionApp"
*.host1.app[0].connectAddress = "host2"
*.host1.app[0].connectPort = 1000
*.host1.app[0].tOpen = 0.1s
# The data transfer begins well after the handshake has recovered from the lost SYN.
*.host1.app[0].tSend = 10s
*.host1.app[0].sendBytes = 500B
*.host1.app[0].tClose = -1s
*.host2.numApps = 1
*.host2.app[0].typename = "TcpSinkApp"
*.host2.app[0].localPort = 1000

%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST rfc6298_timeout_after_lost_syn: PASS
